# Per-worktree provenance marker (worktree-reaper, issue #673) — one per worktree
# root, never committed; must stay ignored so it never dirties the reaper clean-check.
# The trailing * also covers the transient .tmp.<pid> write file (atomic write + mv).
.jbl-worktree.json*

# Environment files (dev secrets)
# `*.env` subsumes the bare `.env` below — gitignore's `*` matches the empty string — but the
# `.local` lines do NOT follow from it and are still load-bearing. The bare `.env` is kept as
# documentation of intent, not as a rule that does work.
#
# WHY `*.env` EXISTS: `vpssecrets.env` sat untracked-but-trackable in the root of a PUBLIC repo
# on 2026-08-12 holding the box's crypto values and database passwords, because `.env` alone
# matches a file NAMED .env. Nothing leaked. "It is a .env, therefore it is ignored" is an
# assumption a human will make again; this makes it true.
#
# THE RULE IS NOT FREE, and the cost is the opposite of the one you would guess: a stray secrets
# file used to announce itself as `?? vpssecrets.env` in `git status`, and now it is silent. That
# trade is right in a public repo — silent beats committable — but it means the working tree no
# longer tells you the file is there. Which is why this is defence in depth and not the control:
# a secrets file does not belong inside the tree at all, ignored or not, since `git add -f` and
# every tool that walks the tree ignore this file. (Verify the tracked set with
# `git ls-files | grep '\.env$'` rather than trusting a count written here.)
*.env
.env
.env.local
.env.*.local
appsettings.Local.json

# RSA keys and other local secrets (never commit)
.secrets/

# #560 (ADR 0091) — SCB client certificates + provisioning docs (personal credential, never commit).
# gitleaks is text/regex-based and can miss a binary .pfx, so this ignore is the load-bearing guard
# against an accidental `git add` of the cert (contract breach + API-Id ban risk).
*.pfx
*.p12
docs/scb/

